data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11 (shipping with .NET 11), the **sync-over-async opt-in for the Cosmos DB provider is removed entirely**. The Cosmos DB SDK is inherently async-only, so EF Core no longer offers the workaround.

Every synchronous LINQ operator and `SaveChanges()` call against the Cosmos DB provider will throw an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

### What to change

| Sync (breaks) | Async (required) |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- All callers up the chain must also become `async`/`await` — this tends to "spread" through the codebase.
- `Add()` stays synchronous (it only tracks in-memory); the actual I/O happens in `SaveChangesAsync()`.
- Remove any `CosmosOptionsExtension` or `EnableSyncOverAsync()` configuration — it's no longer recognized and will throw at startup.
